01:46:28,044 --> 01:46:38,558
directly to Redis, are your own last mile, and they're exactly, precisely where a

604
01:46:38,858 --> 01:46:47,870
surprising number of otherwise well designed systems quietly, completely drop the ball.

605
01:46:48,170 --> 01:46:58,684
Let's talk honestly about why this specific gap happens so consistently across so many

606
01:46:58,984 --> 01:47:00,486
real teams.

607
01:47:00,786 --> 01:47:11,300
External TLS is genuinely, immediately visible, browsers actively, visibly warn users the moment it's

608
01:47:11,600 --> 01:47:22,114
missing or broken, and it's the very first, most obvious thing any external customer

609
01:47:22,414 --> 01:47:29,924
or partner will ever actually notice and complain about directly.

610
01:47:30,224 --> 01:47:40,738
Internal TLS, between your own services, inside your own private network, is completely invisible

611
01:47:41,038 --> 01:47:46,295
to literally everyone except your own team.

612
01:47:46,595 --> 01:47:57,109
Nothing external ever, visibly breaks if it's quietly missing, right up until, one day,

613
01:47:57,409 --> 01:48:07,923
someone with legitimate network access inside your cluster runs a simple packet capture and

614
01:48:08,223 --> 01:48:18,737
discovers your raw, live database credentials and query results flowing by, completely readable, in

615
01:48:19,037 --> 01:48:21,290
plain, unencrypted text.

616
01:48:21,590 --> 01:48:32,104
Here's the mental model for understanding `verify-full` mode specifically for PostgreSQL, because the specific

617
01:48:32,404 --> 01:48:42,918
SSL mode you actually choose here genuinely matters enormously, considerably more than most engineers

618
01:48:43,218 --> 01:48:44,720
initially assume.

619
01:48:45,020 --> 01:48:50,277
`require` alone only forces basic encryption itself.

620
01:48:50,577 --> 01:49:01,091
It says absolutely nothing whatsoever about actually verifying you're genuinely, provably connecting to the

621
01:49:01,391 --> 01:49:07,399
real, legitimate database server you actually intend to.

622
01:49:07,699 --> 01:49:18,213
`verify-full` adds the missing, critical second half of that entire equation, actively checking the

623
01:49:18,513 --> 01:49:29,027
server's own certificate against a trusted certificate authority and additionally confirming the specific hostname

624
01:49:29,327 --> 01:49:31,580
genuinely, correctly matches.

625
01:49:31,880 --> 01:49:42,394
Without that second half fully in place, you're technically encrypted, sure, but potentially, quite

626
01:49:42,694 --> 01:49:53,208
genuinely encrypted directly to an attacker who's successfully, actively positioned themselves directly in the

627
01:49:53,508 --> 01:50:04,022
middle of that connection, which is, in every practical, meaningful sense, barely any real

628
01:50:04,322 --> 01:50:06,575
protection at all.

629
01:50:06,875 --> 01:50:17,389
Let's walk through the actual application code, because downloading and properly trusting the RDS

630
01:50:17,689 --> 01:50:28,203
certificate authority bundle specifically is a small, easily overlooked detail that quietly, completely determines

631
01:50:28,503 --> 01:50:39,017
whether verify-full mode actually genuinely works as intended, or silently, invisibly fails open somewhere

632
01:50:39,317 --> 01:50:46,827
in a way that's considerably harder to notice and catch.

633
01:50:47,127 --> 01:50:57,641
AWS publishes and rotates its own RDS certificate authority bundle on a regular, defined

634
01:50:57,941 --> 01:51:08,455
schedule, and your application specifically needs a genuinely current copy of it to correctly,

635
01:51:08,755 --> 01:51:11,008
successfully verify connections.

636
01:51:11,308 --> 01:51:21,823
This is exactly, precisely why the download happens automatically, on demand, inside your actual

637
01:51:22,123 --> 01:51:32,637
application code itself, rather than being manually, informally pasted in once by a single

638
01:51:32,937 --> 01:51:43,451
engineer and then, inevitably, quietly forgotten about and left to gradually, silently go stale

639
01:51:43,751 --> 01:51:46,004
over real time.

640
01:51:46,304 --> 01:51:56,818
Here's the mental model for Redis specifically, because it genuinely needs both encryption and

641
01:51:57,118 --> 01:52:07,632
a completely separate, distinct authentication mechanism, and confusing those two entirely different concerns is

642
01:52:07,932 --> 01:52:13,189
a genuinely common, easy mistake to make.

643
01:52:13,489 --> 01:52:24,003
`transit_encryption_enabled` protects the actual conversation itself from being casually read by an eavesdropper somewhere

644
01:52:24,303 --> 01:52:26,556
on the network.

645
01:52:26,856 --> 01:52:37,370
The separate auth token is a completely different concern entirely, it verifies your client

646
01:52:37,670 --> 01:52:48,184
is genuinely, actually allowed to talk to this specific Redis instance at all, in

647
01:52:48,484 --> 01:52:55,243
the first place, before any conversation even meaningfully begins.

648
01:52:55,543 --> 01:53:06,057
Encryption without authentication means a random, unauthorized pod on your network can't read the

649
01:53:06,357 --> 01:53:16,871
actual conversation as it happens, but might still, technically, be freely allowed to directly

650
01:53:17,171 --> 01:53:20,926
connect and issue arbitrary commands.

651
01:53:21,226 --> 01:53:31,740
Authentication without encryption means only legitimately authorized clients can connect, but a network based

652
01:53:32,040 --> 01:53:38,799
eavesdropper could still passively read everything flowing by regardless.

653
01:53:39,099 --> 01:53:49,613
You genuinely need both properties working together, simultaneously, for either one to actually mean

654
01:53:49,913 --> 01:53:53,668
very much on its own.

655
01:53:53,968 --> 01:54:04,482
Here's a genuinely useful verification habit worth building directly into your regular routine, the

656
01:54:04,782 --> 01:54:11,541
same discipline we've applied consistently throughout this entire phase.

657
01:54:11,841 --> 01:54:22,355
Query `pg_stat_ssl` directly and confirm, concretely, that active connections genuinely show TLS 1.3 with

658
01:54:22,655 --> 01:54:33,169
a strong, modern cipher, rather than simply, comfortably trusting that your correctly written Terraform

659
01:54:33,469 --> 01:54:43,983
configuration definitely, automatically means the actual live, running connections are behaving exactly as you

660
01:54:44,283 --> 01:54:47,287
honestly intend them to.

661
01:54:47,587 --> 01:54:58,101
The mental model to carry forward into our final lecture, on encryption audit evidence.

662
01:54:58,401 --> 01:55:08,915
Every single control we've carefully built throughout this entire phase, KMS, rotation, TLS 1.3,

663
01:55:09,215 --> 01:55:19,729
mTLS, cert-manager, column-level encryption, internal TLS, is currently invisible, entirely, completely, to anyone outside

664
01:55:20,029 --> 01:55:30,543
your own team, unless you can actually, concretely, convincingly prove it, on demand, to

665
01:55:30,843 --> 01:55:38,353
a genuine stranger who has real, professional reason to ask.

666
01:55:38,653 --> 01:55:49,167
Let's make the last mile risk even more concrete with a realistic, honest scenario

667
01:55:49,467 --> 01:55:52,471
worth walking through directly.

668
01:55:52,771 --> 01:56:03,285
A new engineer joins your team and is asked to quickly debug a genuine,

669
01:56:03,585 --> 01:56:05,838
real production issue.

670
01:56:06,138 --> 01:56:16,652
